Authored chapters land here as `s<season>-ch<n>.json`, one file per chapter,
matching `chapterFileSchema` in packages/core/src/content-schema.ts.

No chapter has been AUTHORED yet. Every gate that reads this folder declares an
empty authored set rather than passing silently (R61).

The moment a file lands here it is held to the full child-facing bar: validate:content
(schema), scan:vocab (banned vocabulary), check:reading-age (sentence length against the
chapter's own readingAgeTarget), scan:chapter-style (the Anti-Marker Law), and
verify:chapters (seeded words resolve, clueTap item is LIVE, released implies audio).

SEASON `fixture` IS RESERVED, and holds the one file here that is not authored content:
`s9-ch01-gate-fixture.json`. It exists so the e2e can prove that a GATED chapter renders,
not merely that the reader renders something — `e2e` is an excluded segment in the
vocabulary scanner, so a fixture kept under apps/web/e2e reaches the reader without
passing a single copy gate. This one passes all five, at status `review`, which is both
the status a writer's first chapter actually has and the reason this file can sit here
safely: production serves `released` only, so it cannot reach a child. That rule is
itself asserted, in apps/web/e2e/story-gated-chapter.spec.ts, against two servers.

Its id cannot collide with a real chapter, and the reader prints the season as the
page's kicker, so anyone who reaches it in staging reads "FIXTURE" above the title.

It points at the PERMANENT seeded fixtures — `zz-e2e-fixture-word` and `zz-e2e-fixture-item`,
both LIVE, both seeded before the chapter gates run — so it satisfies verify:chapters without
any spec creating or mutating a row. Never point a chapter here at real vault content: a
teardown that demotes a real card is the incident those fixtures exist to stop.

story-lit.spec.ts still uses its own chapter under apps/web/e2e/fixtures/chapters/, because it
still builds its own LIVE rows at test time. Once it points at the seeded fixtures instead,
that chapter can move here too and the split goes away.
